RVC torch-free voice-conversion worker (issue #144)
===================================================

The ephemeral, warm-load-once subprocess engine that converts a Kokoro (or any)
source WAV into one of our trained RVC voices, torch-free. This is the shared
engine the future Go decorator (#145) will drive; this deliverable is the Python
worker + wrapper + venv + parity gate only. No Go code.

Files
-----
  scripts/rvc                  bash wrapper — activates .venv-rvc, execs the runner
  scripts/rvc_worker.py        the runner (DSP §4 port + protocol loop)
  scripts/rvc-requirements.txt  exact-pinned torch-free deps
  tests/rvc_parity/            standalone parity + contract gate (make rvc-parity)

Setup
-----
  make rvc-worker-venv         # builds .venv-rvc (python3.12), installs pinned deps,
                               # asserts torch is absent

  Requires the #143 export artifacts on disk under assets/rvc-models/ (gitignored,
  regenerable via `make rvc-export`, backed up to the vd09-projects/rvc-voices HF
  repo). The worker fails cleanly at startup if a shared artifact is missing.

Wire protocol (v1) — the contract #145 binds to
------------------------------------------------
Read stdin line by line. Each line is shlex-split into EXACTLY 5 positional tokens:

    <in> <out> <voice> <index_rate> <pitch>

  <in>          source WAV path (any sr/channels; decoded + resampled to 16 kHz)
  <out>         output WAV path (40 kHz mono s16le; written atomically)
  <voice>       roster slug: cool-jahns | confident-neal
  <index_rate>  float in [0.0, 1.0]; the LINE's value is authoritative
  <pitch>       int; MUST be 0 in phase one (non-zero rejected — no transpose)

Response — exactly ONE physical line per request (response count == request count):

    OK <out>                     success; the WAV now exists at <out>
    ERR <category> <message>     recoverable failure; the loop continues

  <category> ∈ { bad-args | bad-voice | read-failed | infer-failed | write-failed }

  Grammar of what can appear on a response line:
    ┌────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ OK  <out>  │ conversion succeeded, file written                          │
    │ ERR bad-args      │ wrong token count / bad index_rate / non-int or ≠0 pitch │
    │ ERR bad-voice     │ <voice> not in the roster                            │
    │ ERR read-failed   │ <in> missing / undecodable                           │
    │ ERR infer-failed  │ a stage raised during conversion (recoverable)       │
    │ ERR write-failed  │ atomic write to <out> failed (no partial left)       │
    └────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  <message> has newlines/CRs collapsed to spaces and is capped at 300 chars, so an
  ERR is ALWAYS one physical line. A newline/CR-bearing <in> or <out> is REJECTED
  up front as `ERR bad-args` (paths must be single-line), so the `OK <out>` line
  echoes the REAL written path verbatim — the OK name and the file on disk can never
  diverge. #145 branches on the <category> token alone; an unknown category
  (forward-compat) should be treated as fatal.

Forward-compat: the 5-token line is positional and append-only. Never reorder or
repurpose a token; future capability is a trailing optional token with an
engine-faithful default. The ERR category set is append-only.

Process semantics
-----------------
  EOF on stdin                  -> clean exit 0 (models freed; 0 idle RAM)
  BrokenPipeError writing out   -> clean exit 0 (parent closed the pipe)
  MemoryError / catchable fatal -> stderr + nonzero exit (NOT a per-block ERR)
  missing artifact / torch      -> stderr + nonzero exit (startup fatal)
  native fault (segfault)       -> nonzero signal exit (uncatchable in Python)

Timeouts (60 s/block, 10 min/wall) are the Go caller's (#145) job, not the worker's.

Models load once; `LOAD <kind> <slug>` prints to stderr exactly once per (kind,
voice) — the warm-load-once proof the parity gate asserts.

Trust assumptions (phase-one)
-----------------------------
The worker trusts its caller. `_atomic_write_wav` creates parent directories from
the caller-supplied <out> path with NO containment check — a <out> of
`../../elsewhere/x.wav` (or an absolute path) writes there, and <in> is read from
wherever it points. This is ACCEPTABLE under phase-one local-only operation with a
single trusted producer (today a human via `make rvc-convert`; tomorrow the #145 Go
decorator, itself the trust boundary). If #145 ever fronts a less-trusted producer
(paths derived from untrusted network input), add a path-jail — resolve + confine
<in>/<out> under an allowed root — THEN; it is deliberately not paid for now. The
local-only secrets caveat still applies: a source clip is read, and its conversion
written, wherever told.

Env switches (both first-class, default OFF)
--------------------------------------------
  OMP_NUM_THREADS=1   single-thread ORT (intra_op=1). Pre-wired fallback for the
                      faiss+ORT OpenMP-contention case. Default is ORT multithread
                      (safe — torch, the other libomp, is never imported).
  RVC_SEED=<int>      seed numpy RNG so the external net_g flow noise (`rnd`) is
                      deterministic — used by `make rvc-parity`. The internal
                      net_g NSF-dither RandomNormalLike is ORT-generated and NOT
                      seedable; it is the ~0.0009-RMS parity floor.

Deferred (documented, NOT shipped)
----------------------------------
  --mmap   a memory-mapped model-load flag. Full-load is the sole shipped path and
           what parity judges (review S7). Not wired — do not add a half-flag.

Manual smoke
------------
  make rvc-convert VOICE=cool-jahns IN=some.wav OUT=/tmp/out.wav   # by ear

Parity gate
-----------
  make rvc-parity   # per-stage refio corr ≥0.999 + full-pipeline log-mel corr
                    # ≥0.98 (both voices) + torch-free banner + protocol-loop
                    # contract + arg validation + atomic write + WAV format.
                    # Needs the committed fixtures (tests/rvc_parity/gen_targets.py).
